WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:14.310
[Ashlee] We're in a car train.

00:00:14.310 --> 00:00:16.250
It's going up the Swiss Alps.

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:19.960
There's no one at the wheel.

00:00:21.880 --> 00:00:25.697
Oh, there he is.

00:00:25.697 --> 00:00:29.953
It's a little scary in here now.

00:00:33.225 --> 00:00:34.454
Come on, guy.

00:00:34.454 --> 00:00:37.037
I've got a helicopter to catch.

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:02.430
- Travel to Switzerland,

00:01:02.430 --> 00:01:04.473
and an obvious thought will hit you.

00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:08.193
The country can feel too perfect.

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:13.263
The cities.

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:15.633
The watches.

00:01:18.090 --> 00:01:19.683
The cities with watches.

00:01:23.070 --> 00:01:23.903
The lakes.

00:01:25.740 --> 00:01:26.733
The mountains.

00:01:28.950 --> 00:01:30.063
Roger Federer,

00:01:32.190 --> 00:01:33.453
the ideal man.

00:01:37.620 --> 00:01:41.640
It's all so well made, either
by humans or by nature,

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:44.133
it's almost boring.

00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:49.050
And it's true that there
is a museum-like quality

00:01:49.050 --> 00:01:49.923
to Switzerland.

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:55.210
It feels like they figured everything out

00:01:58.290 --> 00:01:59.283
then stopped.

00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:05.853
But let me offer an alternative view.

00:02:10.710 --> 00:02:14.280
Because in Switzerland,
you can also find the shock

00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:16.323
of the new and unexpected.

00:02:17.550 --> 00:02:20.670
The country aims a large portion
of its considerable wealth

00:02:20.670 --> 00:02:23.523
at universities and research institutions.

00:02:24.660 --> 00:02:27.000
These places are doing fantastic work

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:30.603
in fields like robotics, AI, and medicine.

00:02:31.830 --> 00:02:35.490
And Switzerland is also a
country that welcomes immigrants,

00:02:35.490 --> 00:02:37.950
at least crazy, smart immigrants

00:02:37.950 --> 00:02:40.500
and helps them create
startups from their research.

00:02:41.970 --> 00:02:42.933
Let's go meet one.

00:02:50.130 --> 00:02:52.980
- Wow.

00:02:52.980 --> 00:02:53.980
They are everywhere.

00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:02.550
- [Ashlee] Like if I did hit one,

00:03:02.550 --> 00:03:03.427
would it just try to get back?

00:03:03.427 --> 00:03:05.310
- You mean, like this?
- Yeah.

00:03:09.554 --> 00:03:11.520
- And it just finds its location again.

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:12.423
- Yeah, exactly.

00:03:18.390 --> 00:03:19.740
- The worst thing that could happen

00:03:19.740 --> 00:03:22.740
is that somehow the
propeller hits your eyeball.

00:03:22.740 --> 00:03:23.820
- Yeah.

00:03:23.820 --> 00:03:24.963
Why did you say that?

00:03:32.730 --> 00:03:33.563
- [Ashlee] On this show,

00:03:33.563 --> 00:03:35.763
we tend to feature people
who are exceptional.

00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:39.240
But the man behind these drones

00:03:39.240 --> 00:03:41.883
is unusual even among the exceptional.

00:03:45.150 --> 00:03:46.650
- My name is Raffaello D'Andrea,

00:03:46.650 --> 00:03:48.270
everybody calls me Raff.

00:03:48.270 --> 00:03:50.070
I'm a professor at ETH Zurich

00:03:50.070 --> 00:03:52.953
and I'm also the CEO
and founder of Verity.

00:03:57.810 --> 00:03:59.643
- [Ashlee] Raff is part math genius,

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:03.450
part robotics pioneer,

00:04:03.450 --> 00:04:05.190
and part artist.

00:04:05.190 --> 00:04:08.370
- Oh yeah, you can actually
give it a good shove.

00:04:08.370 --> 00:04:09.660
- Yep, it balances.

00:04:09.660 --> 00:04:11.190
- That's amazing.

00:04:11.190 --> 00:04:12.360
- And not only can it balance,

00:04:12.360 --> 00:04:15.060
it can actually rotate and
balance at the same time.

00:04:15.060 --> 00:04:15.893
- [Ashlee] No way.

00:04:15.893 --> 00:04:17.910
- It's very delicate what it does, so.

00:04:17.910 --> 00:04:20.593
- I'm scared to move.

00:04:24.630 --> 00:04:25.803
The cube was cool,

00:04:27.090 --> 00:04:28.353
then he supersized it.

00:04:29.520 --> 00:04:32.580
- [Raff] So this is the balancing cube.

00:04:32.580 --> 00:04:34.470
- And the goal here is for this to stay

00:04:34.470 --> 00:04:36.090
upright and balanced.

00:04:36.090 --> 00:04:36.923
- Exactly.

00:04:37.830 --> 00:04:39.900
It changes its position.

00:04:39.900 --> 00:04:41.850
So it changes the center of mass.

00:04:41.850 --> 00:04:43.920
- Yeah.
- To compensate for movement.

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:47.820
- So inside I'm seeing like
motors and circuit boards

00:04:47.820 --> 00:04:49.950
- There's motors, there's sensors.

00:04:49.950 --> 00:04:51.180
And actually what you don't see,

00:04:51.180 --> 00:04:53.680
is that these are all
communicating to each other.

00:04:55.260 --> 00:04:56.730
- Raff's talent for engineering

00:04:56.730 --> 00:04:59.013
has made him a very wealthy man.

00:04:59.910 --> 00:05:03.090
In 2003, he co-founded Kiva Systems

00:05:03.090 --> 00:05:07.050
a robotics company specializing
in warehouse automation.

00:05:07.050 --> 00:05:12.050
In 2012, Amazon acquired
Kiva for almost $800 million.

00:05:14.970 --> 00:05:17.130
For someone who's into
so many things as you,

00:05:17.130 --> 00:05:18.240
it must have changed your life

00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:20.040
to have Amazon buy the company

00:05:20.040 --> 00:05:22.800
and then you get to chase
whatever you want really.

00:05:22.800 --> 00:05:24.840
- Yeah, I think I've been fortunate

00:05:24.840 --> 00:05:27.566
that I've been able to
do what I want to do.

00:05:30.150 --> 00:05:32.304
This is how we tested the first versions.

00:05:34.380 --> 00:05:35.220
Yeah, that's me right there.

00:05:35.220 --> 00:05:36.053
- [Ashlee] Okay.

00:05:37.500 --> 00:05:40.083
Raff discovered his passion early in life.

00:05:43.440 --> 00:05:45.540
He was born in Italy,

00:05:45.540 --> 00:05:47.730
then moved to Canada at 10

00:05:47.730 --> 00:05:51.870
and quickly developed into
a budding mad scientist.

00:05:51.870 --> 00:05:53.190
- I did some stupid things.

00:05:53.190 --> 00:05:54.480
I learned about pressure

00:05:54.480 --> 00:05:57.810
by jumping into a swimming
pool with a hose in my mouth.

00:05:57.810 --> 00:05:58.950
I jumped off the roof of my house

00:05:58.950 --> 00:06:00.600
with an umbrella, a lawn umbrella,

00:06:00.600 --> 00:06:02.460
because I wanted it to
be like a parachute.

00:06:02.460 --> 00:06:03.803
- Did you break anything on that one?

00:06:03.803 --> 00:06:04.920
- No, I didn't, it was a, you know,

00:06:04.920 --> 00:06:07.260
a Canada bungalow, only 10 feet.

00:06:07.260 --> 00:06:08.093
- Yeah.

00:06:08.093 --> 00:06:09.930
- But what a great way to learn something

00:06:09.930 --> 00:06:12.960
by actually putting
yourself in the experiment.

00:06:12.960 --> 00:06:15.810
- He miraculously survived to adulthood,

00:06:15.810 --> 00:06:18.270
worked and taught in the United States,

00:06:18.270 --> 00:06:21.510
and then for good reason,
settled in Zurich.

00:06:21.510 --> 00:06:24.720
I've heard Zurich is known as Drone Valley

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:26.490
or something like that, or is that right?

00:06:26.490 --> 00:06:29.340
- Yeah, there is a lot of activity

00:06:29.340 --> 00:06:32.010
in autonomous systems in general.

00:06:32.010 --> 00:06:32.880
Switzerland is known

00:06:32.880 --> 00:06:35.370
for doing a lot of cool stuff with drones.

00:06:35.370 --> 00:06:37.980
When we started doing
drone research in 2008,

00:06:37.980 --> 00:06:40.080
that was still kind of
early days for drones

00:06:40.080 --> 00:06:41.280
and there we really aimed

00:06:41.280 --> 00:06:43.680
at pushing the boundary
of autonomous flight.

00:06:43.680 --> 00:06:45.300
So we did a lot of crazy stuff

00:06:45.300 --> 00:06:47.490
with pole balancing and playing ping pong

00:06:47.490 --> 00:06:49.830
and a whole bunch of other fun things.

00:06:49.830 --> 00:06:53.460
It was a way to motivate the researchers.

00:06:53.460 --> 00:06:55.530
If we can make something
that makes people go,

00:06:55.530 --> 00:06:58.480
wow that's amazing, then we
know we've pushed the boundary.

00:07:03.150 --> 00:07:05.400
- [Ashlee] To commercialize
his acrobatic drones,

00:07:05.400 --> 00:07:09.123
Raff started a new company
called Verity in 2014.

00:07:10.110 --> 00:07:11.943
They made their artsy machines,

00:07:14.730 --> 00:07:17.280
and then oddly, began chasing that sweet,

00:07:17.280 --> 00:07:20.280
sweet warehouse management money as well,

00:07:20.280 --> 00:07:22.470
hoping to replace fallible humans

00:07:22.470 --> 00:07:24.363
with a fully automated system.

00:07:25.260 --> 00:07:27.870
- People hate doing inventory control.

00:07:27.870 --> 00:07:30.690
It is a detested task.

00:07:30.690 --> 00:07:32.760
As a result, people don't do inventory

00:07:32.760 --> 00:07:34.470
nearly as often as they should,

00:07:34.470 --> 00:07:37.080
and they live with these errors.

00:07:37.080 --> 00:07:38.720
In a warehouse that has, you know,

00:07:38.720 --> 00:07:41.460
a $100 million of throughput

00:07:41.460 --> 00:07:44.160
and there's a 3% or 4%
error in that warehouse,

00:07:44.160 --> 00:07:45.720
that's a significant amount.

00:07:45.720 --> 00:07:46.553
- Sure.

00:07:47.507 --> 00:07:49.560
To help fix some of the costly mistakes,

00:07:49.560 --> 00:07:51.780
Verity can send over some of its drones

00:07:51.780 --> 00:07:53.700
to work the night shift.

00:07:53.700 --> 00:07:56.250
- The drones fly when
there's no one there.

00:07:56.250 --> 00:07:57.990
And then the system synchronizes

00:07:57.990 --> 00:08:01.590
with the warehouse management
system and then creates a list

00:08:01.590 --> 00:08:04.293
of all the things that need to be fixed.

00:08:06.360 --> 00:08:09.693
- [Ashlee] Raff showed me the
aerial auditors in action.

00:08:14.370 --> 00:08:16.110
- [Raff] It's doing these
very fine adjustments

00:08:16.110 --> 00:08:17.640
to make sure that it's in the right spot

00:08:17.640 --> 00:08:19.523
of where it needs to be to take the image.

00:08:23.640 --> 00:08:24.990
- [Ashlee] Verity's warehouse drones

00:08:24.990 --> 00:08:26.520
have been pretty successful,

00:08:26.520 --> 00:08:30.030
landing customers like Ikea and Samsung.

00:08:30.030 --> 00:08:34.740
They raised a $32 million
funding round in early 2023.

00:08:34.740 --> 00:08:39.150
And these drones can do a
lot more than scan QR codes.

00:08:39.150 --> 00:08:40.890
- Our entertainment business,

00:08:40.890 --> 00:08:43.710
it's less than 10% of what we do,

00:08:43.710 --> 00:08:45.150
but it's an important part of what we do.

00:08:45.150 --> 00:08:47.040
It's in our DNA, you know,

00:08:47.040 --> 00:08:49.203
doing things when
failure is not an option.

00:08:50.550 --> 00:08:52.680
- [Ashlee] To Raff, whether
he's flying his flock

00:08:52.680 --> 00:08:55.500
past 10,000 boxes of liquid soap

00:08:55.500 --> 00:08:59.700
or over the heads of 10,000
spellbound spectators,

00:08:59.700 --> 00:09:01.350
very little changes.

00:09:01.350 --> 00:09:02.730
- [Raff] You know, for
me, the act of creation

00:09:02.730 --> 00:09:05.040
is what I really enjoy.
- Yeah.

00:09:05.040 --> 00:09:07.200
- [Raff] And the nice
thing about technology

00:09:07.200 --> 00:09:09.240
and specifically robotics and AI,

00:09:09.240 --> 00:09:11.130
is that whether it's art,

00:09:11.130 --> 00:09:12.630
whether it's industrial practice,

00:09:12.630 --> 00:09:14.010
whether it's research,

00:09:14.010 --> 00:09:16.590
it's all about taking something

00:09:16.590 --> 00:09:18.450
and making it do motion

00:09:18.450 --> 00:09:20.913
that hasn't been done
before through mathematics.

00:09:24.150 --> 00:09:25.020
This is only 16.

00:09:25.020 --> 00:09:26.490
Can you imagine when you have 200 of them?

00:09:26.490 --> 00:09:27.323
- Yeah.

00:09:28.288 --> 00:09:30.750
I feel like I'm in "A
Midsummer Night's Dream".

00:09:30.750 --> 00:09:31.583
- [Raff] It is magical.

00:09:31.583 --> 00:09:32.490
You know, we take it for granted

00:09:32.490 --> 00:09:34.770
'cause we do it all the
time, but it is amazing

00:09:34.770 --> 00:09:37.110
that intelligent monkeys
can do this. Right?

00:09:37.110 --> 00:09:38.983
- Yeah.

00:09:48.270 --> 00:09:49.103
It could be the case

00:09:49.103 --> 00:09:51.663
that crazy, flying robots
don't do it for you.

00:09:52.530 --> 00:09:54.326
I understand,

00:09:54.326 --> 00:09:55.159
ish.

00:09:58.740 --> 00:10:00.090
And so I have prepared for you

00:10:00.090 --> 00:10:02.310
something more sci-fi,

00:10:02.310 --> 00:10:03.720
more medical,

00:10:03.720 --> 00:10:05.943
more epidermis.

00:10:07.260 --> 00:10:09.210
Welcome to Cutiss,

00:10:09.210 --> 00:10:12.513
purveyors of the finest
lab grown human skin.

00:10:14.070 --> 00:10:16.800
Founder Daniela Marino
is another immigrant

00:10:16.800 --> 00:10:20.310
turning Swiss R &amp; D into a startup.

00:10:20.310 --> 00:10:24.150
She formed Cutiss in
2017 hoping to transform

00:10:24.150 --> 00:10:26.880
the way we treat severe skin damage.

00:10:26.880 --> 00:10:29.100
If I had a burn in an accident

00:10:29.100 --> 00:10:32.070
what would be the standard
thing that happens today?

00:10:32.070 --> 00:10:34.530
- So if you today get burned severely,

00:10:34.530 --> 00:10:39.120
so you lost quite a substantial
amount of your skin,

00:10:39.120 --> 00:10:41.160
you are in danger of life first thing.

00:10:41.160 --> 00:10:44.433
So you are admitted in very
specialized burn units.

00:10:47.220 --> 00:10:49.050
When your body's ready and out of danger

00:10:49.050 --> 00:10:51.720
of risking your life, autografting starts.

00:10:51.720 --> 00:10:55.440
Autografting means that
the surgeon takes skin

00:10:55.440 --> 00:10:57.960
from somewhere else, not burnt,

00:10:57.960 --> 00:11:01.110
on your body, and puts it on the wounds.

00:11:01.110 --> 00:11:02.460
If the wounds are very large

00:11:02.460 --> 00:11:05.970
it is a clinical challenge
to actually find enough skin.

00:11:05.970 --> 00:11:09.420
So what we do is trying
to shift the paradigm

00:11:09.420 --> 00:11:12.930
towards saying, okay, give me
a little piece from somewhere,

00:11:12.930 --> 00:11:14.880
and I'll take it to the
lab, and I'll make it bigger

00:11:14.880 --> 00:11:16.080
and ship it to you back.

00:11:16.950 --> 00:11:19.800
We receive a piece of skin of the patient

00:11:19.800 --> 00:11:21.420
as small as a stamp

00:11:21.420 --> 00:11:25.257
and then we can extract the
stem cells, expand them,

00:11:25.257 --> 00:11:28.743
and then recreate skin using
a bioengineering technique.

00:11:31.860 --> 00:11:33.570
- [Ashlee] When the skin is done growing,

00:11:33.570 --> 00:11:35.040
it looks something like this.

00:11:35.040 --> 00:11:36.938
Mmm, delicious.

00:11:36.938 --> 00:11:37.771
- Mmm.

00:11:42.990 --> 00:11:46.980
- This is both cool and
slightly creepy, isn't it?

00:11:46.980 --> 00:11:47.813
- [Daniela] No.

00:11:48.750 --> 00:11:52.470
You haven't seen anything creepy
for real in your life then.

00:11:52.470 --> 00:11:53.940
- Like how does this look different

00:11:53.940 --> 00:11:55.620
to the final product when it's skin?

00:11:55.620 --> 00:11:57.390
- The final one is a bit thicker

00:11:57.390 --> 00:12:00.450
and it will be much more resistant

00:12:00.450 --> 00:12:03.100
because you have all the
cells that keep it together.

00:12:05.130 --> 00:12:07.830
- I mean, the one major limiting factor,

00:12:07.830 --> 00:12:10.890
it seems right now, is the
pigmentation in the skin.

00:12:10.890 --> 00:12:13.050
What's the plan for addressing that?

00:12:13.050 --> 00:12:15.630
- So at the moment, we don't
have melanocytes in the culture

00:12:15.630 --> 00:12:17.580
which means we are missing the cells

00:12:17.580 --> 00:12:19.020
that produce the melanin

00:12:19.020 --> 00:12:22.020
and we are now having an add-on therapy

00:12:22.020 --> 00:12:24.840
to then simply transfer the melanocytes

00:12:24.840 --> 00:12:27.600
from somewhere colored to the white area

00:12:27.600 --> 00:12:28.620
and produce the melanin

00:12:28.620 --> 00:12:30.753
which will match the color of the patient.

00:12:35.580 --> 00:12:37.380
- [Ashlee] So far, Daniela and her team

00:12:37.380 --> 00:12:40.740
have treated 50 patients
in clinical trials,

00:12:40.740 --> 00:12:43.920
including in one case, a newborn.

00:12:43.920 --> 00:12:46.110
- So this is youngest child ever treated

00:12:46.110 --> 00:12:47.550
with bio engineered skin.

00:12:47.550 --> 00:12:52.350
And you can imagine the
growth of a kid five days old.

00:12:52.350 --> 00:12:53.183
- Yeah.
- And now,

00:12:53.183 --> 00:12:54.420
two and a half years later.

00:12:54.420 --> 00:12:57.150
I mean, the stretch is just humongous

00:12:57.150 --> 00:12:59.477
and it's impressive to see the result.

00:13:01.620 --> 00:13:02.670
- [Ashlee] If all goes well,

00:13:02.670 --> 00:13:04.980
Daniela wants to have
her skin growing machines

00:13:04.980 --> 00:13:06.750
set up around the world,

00:13:06.750 --> 00:13:09.690
not just limited to
hospitals, but in oil rigs,

00:13:09.690 --> 00:13:11.763
commercial kitchens, and war zones.

00:13:12.870 --> 00:13:14.460
As ever with biotech,

00:13:14.460 --> 00:13:16.983
these things take time and lots of money.

00:13:17.820 --> 00:13:21.330
Luckily, Switzerland
adores its science startups

00:13:21.330 --> 00:13:24.210
and lavishes them with resources.

00:13:24.210 --> 00:13:27.720
- You have a a mixture
of brain force here,

00:13:27.720 --> 00:13:29.490
which is incredible.

00:13:29.490 --> 00:13:31.770
And next to this innovative hub

00:13:31.770 --> 00:13:33.810
you have a very strong startup ecosystem.

00:13:33.810 --> 00:13:36.358
So I don't know if I
would've been that successful

00:13:36.358 --> 00:13:39.480
creating Cutiss in my homeland,
Italy, or somewhere else

00:13:39.480 --> 00:13:43.170
because here you really
are nurtured as a startup.

00:13:43.170 --> 00:13:45.600
- Yeah, 'cause there's
not that many countries,

00:13:45.600 --> 00:13:47.340
I think, where you could
raise the amount of money

00:13:47.340 --> 00:13:48.463
it would take to...

00:13:48.463 --> 00:13:51.900
a company that has to have
a few years to get going

00:13:51.900 --> 00:13:53.433
because of all the clinical trials and-

00:13:53.433 --> 00:13:54.510
- Sure, long term investment, for sure.

00:13:54.510 --> 00:13:57.150
- Most places are not willing
to kind of sit through that.

00:13:57.150 --> 00:13:59.253
- This is a very, very expensive journey.

00:14:01.260 --> 00:14:04.410
And there is no plan B, I
mean, it has to be like that.

00:14:04.410 --> 00:14:06.090
So, let's see.

00:14:06.090 --> 00:14:08.947
I hope I continue
raising the money I need.

00:14:10.710 --> 00:14:13.110
- If she's able to bring
the technology to market,

00:14:13.110 --> 00:14:16.113
it could impact millions
upon millions of lives.

00:14:17.160 --> 00:14:19.710
I just hope that after my next adventure

00:14:19.710 --> 00:14:21.441
I don't require her services.

00:14:22.692 --> 00:14:24.246
We're flying straight into the mountains.

00:14:24.246 --> 00:14:29.013
It's scary.

00:14:38.070 --> 00:14:39.513
Zurich is a lovely town.

00:14:43.200 --> 00:14:44.940
But this is Switzerland,

00:14:44.940 --> 00:14:46.923
and there are natural wonders to see.

00:14:48.810 --> 00:14:50.460
I'm told there's nothing like soaring

00:14:50.460 --> 00:14:52.233
around the Alps in a helicopter.

00:14:53.160 --> 00:14:55.440
However, dodging crags in a metal cage

00:14:55.440 --> 00:14:59.130
is somewhat well, extremely terrifying.

00:14:59.130 --> 00:15:01.440
Mostly because I'm not a pilot

00:15:01.440 --> 00:15:03.210
and I don't want to start learning

00:15:03.210 --> 00:15:04.923
at 13,000 feet.

00:15:06.000 --> 00:15:07.440
As it happens, however,

00:15:07.440 --> 00:15:10.230
I am that lucky tourist who knows a guy

00:15:10.230 --> 00:15:14.160
who can make all of my
sightseeing dreams come true.

00:15:14.160 --> 00:15:16.332
- My name is Fabi Riesen.

00:15:16.332 --> 00:15:21.217
I have the pleasure to be the
CEO of Loft Dynamics Company.

00:15:22.710 --> 00:15:25.020
Basically, Loft is producing

00:15:25.020 --> 00:15:27.810
the world's first flight
simulator training device

00:15:27.810 --> 00:15:29.880
using virtual reality technology.

00:15:29.880 --> 00:15:30.713
- [Ashlee] Okay.

00:15:30.713 --> 00:15:31.800
- So it's kind of like a-

00:15:31.800 --> 00:15:34.803
- Truthfully, I'm a bit of
a flight simulator veteran.

00:15:38.880 --> 00:15:41.310
But even at the high end training centers

00:15:41.310 --> 00:15:44.340
pretend flying still has its limitations.

00:15:44.340 --> 00:15:45.660
You go to NASA or something

00:15:45.660 --> 00:15:49.200
and you expect it to be
space agey and cutting edge.

00:15:49.200 --> 00:15:50.850
But the simulators always look

00:15:50.850 --> 00:15:53.850
like they were built in like the 1980s.

00:15:53.850 --> 00:15:55.440
I mean, it seems like technology

00:15:55.440 --> 00:15:57.420
that hasn't changed in a long time.

00:15:57.420 --> 00:15:58.560
- Yeah.

00:15:58.560 --> 00:16:01.500
Those gigantic domes
give you a very nice view

00:16:01.500 --> 00:16:05.640
but the main challenge is
everything outside is projected.

00:16:05.640 --> 00:16:10.230
So if you have two
objects behind each other

00:16:10.230 --> 00:16:12.090
and you move the head,
you kind of like see

00:16:12.090 --> 00:16:14.790
that they are moving against each other.

00:16:14.790 --> 00:16:17.160
On the dome projection, nothing happens.

00:16:17.160 --> 00:16:18.510
With virtual reality,

00:16:18.510 --> 00:16:20.700
this is basically not the case anymore.

00:16:20.700 --> 00:16:24.183
And it's like real from
a visual point of view.

00:16:28.230 --> 00:16:30.360
- [Ashlee] Fabi built
his first VR simulator

00:16:30.360 --> 00:16:31.830
in his living room,

00:16:31.830 --> 00:16:35.313
earning himself the title
of greatest dad of all time.

00:16:36.420 --> 00:16:39.480
He eventually upgraded to a real workshop.

00:16:39.480 --> 00:16:40.683
- [Fabi] Swiss Quality.

00:16:41.550 --> 00:16:44.137
- [Ashlee] Does this all get
made in Switzerland here, or?

00:16:44.137 --> 00:16:46.050
- Yes. Yeah.
- Yeah. Okay.

00:16:46.050 --> 00:16:48.900
The simulators sell for
under a million bucks,

00:16:48.900 --> 00:16:52.020
way less than a traditional dome-type sim,

00:16:52.020 --> 00:16:53.700
which is good because the world

00:16:53.700 --> 00:16:56.340
needs a lot more of these things.

00:16:56.340 --> 00:16:58.590
- Let's talk about the
pilot shortage a bit.

00:16:58.590 --> 00:17:00.900
I mean, it seems like
there's not enough pilots

00:17:00.900 --> 00:17:02.400
and then there's just a ton of training

00:17:02.400 --> 00:17:04.890
both for new pilots and older pilots,

00:17:04.890 --> 00:17:06.630
right, to keep their skills up.

00:17:06.630 --> 00:17:07.920
Is that the general idea?

00:17:07.920 --> 00:17:09.360
- Yes, on one hand

00:17:09.360 --> 00:17:13.770
it's quite expensive to
use the real aircraft.

00:17:13.770 --> 00:17:14.603
- Yeah.
- To do it.

00:17:14.603 --> 00:17:18.270
And on the other hand, the
simulators are also expensive

00:17:18.270 --> 00:17:20.010
and there are simply
not enough simulators.

00:17:20.010 --> 00:17:21.720
So we are continuously

00:17:21.720 --> 00:17:24.303
below the actual need from the pilots.

00:17:25.710 --> 00:17:26.543
- [Ashlee] The true test

00:17:26.543 --> 00:17:29.073
was giving one of these
whirly birds a whirl.

00:17:30.900 --> 00:17:32.283
Get to the chopper!

00:17:33.240 --> 00:17:36.120
- [Tamara] And we're going to
give you a bit of forward speed.

00:17:36.120 --> 00:17:38.820
- This is cool.

00:17:38.820 --> 00:17:40.920
- All right, I'll give you the control

00:17:40.920 --> 00:17:45.000
in 3, 2, 1.

00:17:45.000 --> 00:17:45.963
Your control.

00:17:48.960 --> 00:17:51.360
- Do I need to get higher to get?

00:17:51.360 --> 00:17:52.410
- Yeah, you need to get higher

00:17:52.410 --> 00:17:54.210
because you want to get over that hill.

00:17:54.210 --> 00:17:57.363
so keep pulling, keep
pulling, that's good.

00:17:59.250 --> 00:18:00.150
- [Ashlee] Oh boy.

00:18:02.850 --> 00:18:03.750
Whoa.

00:18:03.750 --> 00:18:05.430
- Okay.
- What do I do?

00:18:05.430 --> 00:18:07.980
- I'm going to take the controls.

00:18:07.980 --> 00:18:09.980
We haven't even started with the pedals.

00:18:11.160 --> 00:18:13.560
- [Ashlee] Wait, what do
the pedals actually do?

00:18:14.910 --> 00:18:16.050
Ooh.

00:18:16.050 --> 00:18:16.883
- [Fabi] You know what?

00:18:16.883 --> 00:18:18.573
Let's make him land the aircraft.

00:18:19.528 --> 00:18:20.361
- [Ashlee] Whoa.

00:18:20.361 --> 00:18:21.194
- Okay. Okay.

00:18:21.194 --> 00:18:22.530
- Whoa, whoa, whoa

00:18:22.530 --> 00:18:25.860
- [Tamara] You're going to
crash into the building, but.

00:18:25.860 --> 00:18:27.983
You're going down really fast.

00:18:27.983 --> 00:18:30.120
Okay.

00:18:30.120 --> 00:18:33.163
Okay, now you managed to crash.

00:18:35.040 --> 00:18:36.630
- As far as I was concerned,

00:18:36.630 --> 00:18:39.870
I had officially graduated
Swiss Flight School.

00:18:39.870 --> 00:18:42.540
I blame you.

00:18:42.540 --> 00:18:45.093
Now it was time to try the real thing.

00:18:48.990 --> 00:18:52.590
So Fabi sent me on a four
hour drive into the Alps.

00:18:52.590 --> 00:18:54.930
Destination, Zermatt.

00:18:54.930 --> 00:18:57.990
A charming mountain town
where we could take off

00:18:57.990 --> 00:19:00.240
and cruise the skies together.

00:19:00.240 --> 00:19:01.680
We're at Air Zermatt.

00:19:01.680 --> 00:19:05.040
The Matterhorn is just over there.

00:19:05.040 --> 00:19:06.360
I'm going to meet up with Fabi

00:19:06.360 --> 00:19:08.576
and I think he's getting here right now.

00:19:14.759 --> 00:19:17.853
You travel in style, huh?

00:19:20.400 --> 00:19:22.980
With a little help from ace pilot, Julie.

00:19:22.980 --> 00:19:25.410
- Like that, good.

00:19:25.410 --> 00:19:28.533
- [Ashlee] It was time to
put my skills to the test.

00:19:41.026 --> 00:19:42.434
- [Julie] You want to try
it a little bit of this?

00:19:42.434 --> 00:19:44.301
- [Ashlee] All right.

00:19:44.301 --> 00:19:46.663
- [Julie] Really, really slowly.

00:19:46.663 --> 00:19:48.183
Okay. Your control.

00:19:49.470 --> 00:19:50.550
- [Fabi] Ashlee, have you realized

00:19:50.550 --> 00:19:52.560
she's not holding anymore, the control?

00:19:52.560 --> 00:19:54.003
It's fully your control.

00:19:55.563 --> 00:19:57.104
- [Ashlee] Don't tell me that, please.

00:19:59.730 --> 00:20:00.750
- [Julie] You're not breathing.

00:20:00.750 --> 00:20:03.313
You have to breathe.

00:20:05.820 --> 00:20:08.430
Have you seen, you're turning yourself?

00:20:08.430 --> 00:20:10.806
- [Ashlee] Did I actually
just do that or did you help?

00:20:10.806 --> 00:20:12.162
- [Julie] Oh, no, no, you did it.

00:20:12.162 --> 00:20:14.001
- [Ashlee] No way.

00:20:14.001 --> 00:20:15.660
All right, I don't like
it when we get so close

00:20:15.660 --> 00:20:18.501
to the mountain though,
then I get freaked out.

00:20:18.501 --> 00:20:22.530
- No problem, you can
turn again to the left.

00:20:22.530 --> 00:20:23.752
I come with you now.

00:20:23.752 --> 00:20:24.692
- Okay. Yes, please.

00:20:24.692 --> 00:20:26.942
- Because we go just there.

00:20:27.997 --> 00:20:31.091
- That was both exciting and
terrifying at the same time.

00:20:38.610 --> 00:20:42.613
I actually flew, it was really cool.

00:20:45.630 --> 00:20:46.680
- [Ashlee] Before heading home,

00:20:46.680 --> 00:20:49.560
Fabi insisted I meet his buddy Mario,

00:20:49.560 --> 00:20:52.230
a mysterious mountain man who's invested

00:20:52.230 --> 00:20:55.080
in some of the best tech
startups in Switzerland,

00:20:55.080 --> 00:20:56.728
including Fabi's.

00:20:56.728 --> 00:20:58.505
- [Fabi] Knock, knock.

00:20:58.505 --> 00:20:59.573
- [Mario] knock, knock.

00:20:59.573 --> 00:21:00.780
- Hey.

00:21:00.780 --> 00:21:01.650
- [Mario] Come in.

00:21:01.650 --> 00:21:04.453
- [Fabi] Mario, how are you? Doing good?

00:21:04.453 --> 00:21:05.590
- Good to see you guys.

00:21:05.590 --> 00:21:06.529
- [Ashlee] Hello. How are you?

00:21:06.529 --> 00:21:07.362
- Pleasure to see you.

00:21:07.362 --> 00:21:08.640
- [Ashlee] Nice to meet you.
- Warm welcome.

00:21:08.640 --> 00:21:11.073
Sit down. Beer, gin and
tonic? What do you like?

00:21:15.810 --> 00:21:17.370
- [Ashlee] Mario had all the trappings

00:21:17.370 --> 00:21:19.680
of an eccentric, free spirit,

00:21:19.680 --> 00:21:24.300
right down to his underground,
secret passageway and spa.

00:21:24.300 --> 00:21:25.470
- [Mario] Watch your head here.

00:21:25.470 --> 00:21:26.610
- [Ashlee] Okay.

00:21:26.610 --> 00:21:30.180
- [Mario] First, this is the wellness area

00:21:30.180 --> 00:21:32.684
and this is where I have
the wine and spirits.

00:21:32.684 --> 00:21:33.984
- [Ashlee] I saw it, yeah.

00:21:35.070 --> 00:21:36.900
So that's the Matterhorn
right out the window.

00:21:36.900 --> 00:21:39.656
- That's the Matterhorn right there.

00:21:39.656 --> 00:21:43.290
And of course I know a lot
of rich people and whatever

00:21:43.290 --> 00:21:45.510
and they are impressed by the house,

00:21:45.510 --> 00:21:47.220
so they offer me tons
of money for this house.

00:21:47.220 --> 00:21:48.540
- Yeah, and what do you tell them?

00:21:48.540 --> 00:21:52.980
- I tell them, not on
sale and never on sale.

00:21:52.980 --> 00:21:57.980
This here is my house and
that's where my spirit is living

00:21:58.290 --> 00:22:00.933
and giving me opportunities
to be creative.

00:22:01.800 --> 00:22:05.880
- Mario was born in Zermatt
and has lived many lives.

00:22:05.880 --> 00:22:07.470
Restaurateur,

00:22:07.470 --> 00:22:09.060
hotelier,

00:22:09.060 --> 00:22:10.530
mountain climber,

00:22:10.530 --> 00:22:12.180
ski instructor

00:22:12.180 --> 00:22:14.760
and eventually tech investor.

00:22:14.760 --> 00:22:16.020
Stirring the fondue,

00:22:16.020 --> 00:22:19.740
I feel like now I get Swiss citizenship.

00:22:19.740 --> 00:22:23.250
If you manage to reach
his fortress of solitude,

00:22:23.250 --> 00:22:25.590
he is a gracious host.

00:22:25.590 --> 00:22:26.423
- Cheers, and thank you
so much for being here.

00:22:26.423 --> 00:22:27.540
- Cheers. So nice meeting you.

00:22:27.540 --> 00:22:29.381
Thanks for inviting us into your home.

00:22:29.381 --> 00:22:30.330
So kind of you.
- Cheers, Fabi.

00:22:30.330 --> 00:22:31.530
- [Ashlee] Appreciate it.

00:22:31.530 --> 00:22:33.660
Mario told me about some of the ventures

00:22:33.660 --> 00:22:35.430
he has in the works.

00:22:35.430 --> 00:22:38.520
- First we make a carbon
fiber out of algae.

00:22:38.520 --> 00:22:39.353
- Okay.

00:22:39.353 --> 00:22:40.950
- We reinforce this with stone.

00:22:40.950 --> 00:22:43.860
- [Ashlee] And then he told
me about them some more.

00:22:43.860 --> 00:22:45.900
- This is not rusting. It cannot.

00:22:45.900 --> 00:22:49.170
You can imagine what impact is
this having on construction.

00:22:49.170 --> 00:22:50.430
- Yeah.
- Also for the ski industry.

00:22:50.430 --> 00:22:52.050
My goal is now to build the house

00:22:52.050 --> 00:22:54.540
completely CO2 neutral, even negative.

00:22:54.540 --> 00:22:55.740
This would be sexy.

00:22:55.740 --> 00:22:56.842
Yeah, it has to be sexy.

00:22:58.230 --> 00:23:01.470
- So you've been to the
North Pole, the South Pole.

00:23:01.470 --> 00:23:03.490
You've climbed the eight
highest peaks, right?

00:23:03.490 --> 00:23:05.730
- Yeah, yeah, traversing Everest.

00:23:05.730 --> 00:23:09.360
When I'm doing expeditions,
I can calm down

00:23:09.360 --> 00:23:13.110
because I think one day I will be crazy.

00:23:13.110 --> 00:23:15.780
I'm enough crazy already, but.

00:23:15.780 --> 00:23:18.480
- I feel like you calm
down by doing more stuff.

00:23:18.480 --> 00:23:22.920
- Yes, but it is running on high speed.

00:23:22.920 --> 00:23:24.300
- Yeah, yeah.
- Because if-

00:23:24.300 --> 00:23:27.030
- [Ashlee] It seemed that Mario
was a kind of spirit animal

00:23:27.030 --> 00:23:29.157
of the Swiss tech scene.

00:23:29.157 --> 00:23:31.037
- [Mario] And with this I can-

00:23:31.037 --> 00:23:33.150
- [Ashlee] Moving at hyper speed,

00:23:33.150 --> 00:23:35.340
embracing new ideas

00:23:35.340 --> 00:23:40.340
flush with cash and perched
atop a majestic alp.

00:23:40.650 --> 00:23:41.793
Bless this country.

00:23:43.950 --> 00:23:46.263
But this wasn't the end
of my Swiss journey.

00:23:47.790 --> 00:23:51.450
It was time to trade in my
helicopter for a bicycle

00:23:51.450 --> 00:23:54.000
and take a mind expanding trip.

00:23:54.000 --> 00:23:54.833
Woo hoo!

